<DocsLayout
  title="Overview"
  description="How OpsRun turns Markdown runbooks into validated, approvable, auditable SSH workflows."
  path="/docs"
>
  <div class="note">
    Start here if you are new:
    <a href="/docs/getting-started">Getting started</a>
    (install → validate → plan).
  </div>

  <p>
    OpsRun is a Go CLI for incident response. Procedures live as Markdown in git. OpsRun
    parses them into a dependency DAG, runs steps over SSH against
    <code>opsrun.yaml</code>, enforces approvals, rolls back on failure, and appends a
    hash-chained audit log.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-you-get">What you get</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>AST-based Markdown parsing</li>
    <li>Concurrent steps with explicit dependencies</li>
    <li>Rolling and canary host strategies</li>
    <li>Approval gates that show command and hosts</li>
    <li>Automatic rollback for completed destructive steps</li>
    <li>Checkpoint and resume</li>
    <li>Secret redaction and mandatory host-key verification by default</li>
  </ul>

